                   "0.14.0.": [
"
This release makes Robot Framework ready for **tests that run for hours or days, in parallel, against real benches** - and keeps the verdict honest.
Existing test suites run unchanged; all new features are opt-in.

**New test status UNKNOWN**

Beside ``PASS``, ``FAIL`` and ``SKIP`` a fourth status ``UNKNOWN`` separates *the product is wrong* from *nothing could be judged*.
A failed assertion is still ``FAIL``; a missing keyword or variable, a failed import, a ``DataError`` or an unexpected exception is ``UNKNOWN``.
Real regressions no longer drown in bench and environment problems.

* Which tests an import error affects:

   .. code::

      python.exe -m robot --importfailure suite testfile.robot     # default: the whole suite is UNKNOWN
      python.exe -m robot --importfailure test  testfile.robot     # only tests using the failed import

* The return code carries both counts, so CI can tell them apart: ``rc = (min(unknown, 14) << 4) | min(failed, 15)``
  (decode with ``failed = rc & 0x0F`` and ``unknown = (rc >> 4) & 0x0F``).

**THREAD keyword: lifecycle scopes and control**

The ``daemon`` flag of a ``THREAD`` block now selects its lifetime: ``True`` stops the thread when its test ends, ``False`` keeps it running
across tests until the suite ends. Stopping is cooperative with a 5 second grace period; a thread that cannot stop is abandoned and reported as ``UNKNOWN``.
New keywords give deterministic control:

   .. code::

      Stop Thread        WORKER    timeout=10 s
      Wait For Thread    WORKER    timeout=1 min

**Thread logs in log.html and the execution timeline**

Keywords, messages and statuses of every ``THREAD`` now appear in ``log.html`` and ``report.html`` as one block at the place the thread started.
The new option ``--timeline`` writes an HTML timeline with one lane per thread on a real time axis; every bar links to its keyword in ``log.html``.

   .. code::

      python.exe -m robot --timeline timeline.html testfile.robot

**New library StateMachine**

Model very long, condition-driven tests as states and guarded transitions. After every transition a checkpoint is written, so a run that crashes
at hour 40 resumes where it stopped instead of starting from zero. Includes per-state timeouts, error routing, machines defined in JSON/YAML files,
several machines at once and per-state statistics.

   .. code::

      Define State         CHARGING    enter=Start Charging    on_error=FAULT
      Define Transition    CHARGING    DISCHARGING    condition=$SOC >= 80
      Run State Machine    initial=INIT    max_duration=48h    checkpoint=${OUTPUT DIR}${/}sm.json

**New library Watchdog**

A supervisor running in a ``THREAD`` block: heartbeat, stall detection and a deadline for any long-running work. When the test stops feeding it,
the watchdog fires, runs a reaction keyword and the test fails with the reason - after minutes, not days.

   .. code::

      Configure Watchdog    stall_timeout=10 min    heartbeat=5 min    on_timeout=Collect Diagnostics

**Flow files: run a test plan drawn as a flowchart**

A flow file (``*.flow.json``) describes a test plan as nodes and edges - setup, gates, bounded loops, recovery, teardown - and is executed directly as a
suite built at run time. No ``.robot`` file to maintain. Gates wait on a condition with a real timeout and report ``UNKNOWN`` when the bench is not ready.
With the new library ``robot.flow.signals`` two flows running as two processes can meet and run in lockstep.

   .. code::

      python.exe -m robot --parser robot.flow --variable BLADE:IVI plan.flow.json
      python.exe -m robot.flow validate plan.flow.json

**Segmented output**

The new option ``--segmentoutput`` seals ``output.xml`` (and thread outputs) into complete, readable segments at a fixed interval.
A crash loses at most one interval of log data; after a normal run the segments are merged back automatically.

   .. code::

      python.exe -m robot --segmentoutput 1h testfile.robot

**Stable memory on long runs**

The message cache, the collected warnings and errors (spilled to disk beyond 10000) and the thread notification queues are bounded,
so memory stays flat over days of execution.

**Bug fixes**

* ``KeyError`` when a thread logs with an own log level above the configured log level (issue #109).
* Crash with ``IndexError`` in variable scopes when threads start and end concurrently on Linux.
* ``output.xml`` that could not be parsed at log levels above ``INFO``, and ``rebot --output`` on outputs that contain threads.

More details can be found in the feature documentation (``docs`` folder of the repository, published as GitHub Pages).
"
